ides greater resistance to displacement—adds substantive value or merely restates what's already covered.\n\nSince the high-confidence material from the earlier run already addresses structural applications more thoroughly and with stronger external support, I don't need to include this medium-tier content. There's no important structured block (list, table, or code) that exists only at medium confidence, so no exception applies. For this slot, all relevant content is high-confidence, so no mixed or medium note is necessary.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the content and preparing to write the files. Let me verify the formatting rules first—I need to use $...$ and $$...$$ for math notation, never the \\(...\\) or \\[...\\] formats. For English output, I'm sticking to ASCII text only, with standard ASCII quotes and hyphens instead of any accented characters or special punctuation.\n\nI'm ready to write out all three slot files along with the compiled document and bibliography.\n\nFor the first slot on metals ranked by melting point, I'm listing iron, copper, and aluminum in descending order by their melting temperatures with proper citations. The second slot covers copper's density advantage over the other two metals, and I'm continuing with the density values for iron and aluminum.\n\nNow I'm explaining how density functions as a critical property in engineering applications—from counterweights and ballast to radiation shielding—where concentrated mass in a compact space is essential. Higher density directly improves attenuation in radiation protection by increasing photon interactions per unit thickness. The thought trails into discussing stiffness and wear resistance properties.\n\nThe knowledge base only provides melting point and density data, so I can't make quantitative comparisons of mechanical performance in load-bearing or wear-intensive applications without additional material property information. I'm now moving into a direct comparison between iron and aluminum b