m the density data into real-world use cases like counterweights and ballast systems.\n\nThe radiation shielding section is also acceptable; \"permitting\" is already the correct form of the verb I need. The point about stiffness and wear resistance being independent of density alone is important to clarify, since density doesn't tell the whole story for mechanical properties.\n\nThe last sentence trails off, but it's making the point that I can only compare these three metals on melting point and density—anything beyond that would require additional property data I don't have in the knowledge base.\n\nNow I'm moving through the comparison list and checking for any problematic language. The statements about iron's thermal stability, aluminum's lower density, and their atomic numbers all check out. Everything looks consistent and accurate.\n\nI'm ready to compile the final document now, organizing it into three sections with a clear title and structure. I'm organizing the content into three slots for the document and need to verify confidence levels across sources. For slot 2, I'm using high-confidence material from one of the runs that covers both identification and structural relevance clearly. For slot 3, I'm checking whether the iron versus aluminum comparison has consistent confidence ratings across my sources. Once I confirm there are no mixed-confidence situations that need flagging, I can proceed with writing out all three slot files in parallel, then compile the final document with the bibliography. Continuing through Slot 2, I'm verifying that the density comparisons and structural application claims all trace back to the same high-confidence sources, with the additional point about stiffness not being a simple function of density also confirmed. ✓\n\nNow moving into Slot 3, I'm checking the melting point values for iron and aluminum, their difference, the density figures, and the density ratio—all of which derive from the knowledge base or are arithmetic calculations from those values. The atomic numbers for both metals are also confirmed across multiple sources.\n\nI'm reconsidering the citation structure to avoid redundancy. Since each bullet point supports different data, the citations belong there, but I should be careful not to repeat the same key at both the introduction and each individual bullet. Let me adjust so the main knowledge base citation appears only where it's most useful, and reserve specific element citations for the data points they directly support. I'm finishing up the atomic number comparison and then moving into a similar structured list for the three metals ranked by melting point, with iron at the top, followed by copper, then aluminum at the lowest. I'm verifying that my content aligns with the academic voice requirements and the specified format for each section—the sorted list for metals, flowing paragraphs for copper's properties, and a concise comparison format for the iron-aluminum differences. My phrasing avoids instructional language and maintains a neutral, descriptive tone throughout.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37469,"cache_read_input_tokens":16297,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":37469,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"04f37524-5119-40e4-97a2-82834c0f4f69"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01SY6tHqMNrG3qTdwyTqgJjH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have all the source data. Now I'll analyze and produce the canonical slot answers.\n\n**Analysis summary:**\n- **Slot 1**: HIGH-confidence ranked list available across multiple sessions (175309_7976, 182635, 203317_2509, 211059_12149)\n- **Slot 2**: HIGH-confidence density identification (all sessions); HIGH-confidence structural relevance paragraph from 182635 (with external citations upgrading from LOW)\n- **Slot 3**: HIGH-confidence comparison from 175309_7976, 182635, 203317_2509, 211059_12149; structured list from 211059_12149\n\n**Citation mapping:**\n- `[KB:metals.md]` → `[@MetalsKB]`\n- `[WEB:.../element/26/iron]` → `[@RSCIron2024]`\n- `[WEB:.../element/29/copper]` → `[@RSCCopper2024]`\n- `[WEB:.../element/13/aluminium]` → `[@RSCAluminium2024]`\n- `[WEB:engineerfix.com/...]` → `[@EngineerFix2024]`\n- `[WEB:lkabminerals.com/...]` → `[@LKABMinerals2024]`\n- `[WEB:physics.nist.gov/...]` → `[@NIST2024]`\n- `[WEB:marshield.com/...]` → `[@MarShield2024]`\n- `[WEB:material-properties.org/...]` → `[@MaterialProperties2024]`\n\nSLOT_PROGRESS: slot-01 :: writing_output"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37469,"cache_read_input_tokens":16297,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":37469,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"73b784a8-4260-412a-9a63-a70233a876a1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01SY6tHqMNrG3qTdwyTqgJjH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Won2bZi9ced1kgzHagwBqu","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-01.md","content":"## SLOT 1: Metals ranked by melting point\n\nThe three metals documented in the knowledge base are listed below in descending order of melting point, from highest to lowest. [@MetalsKB]\n\n1. Iron (Fe): $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCIron2024]\n2. Copper (Cu): $1085\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCCopper2024]\n3. Aluminum (Al): $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCAluminium2024]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37469,"cache_read_input_tokens":16297,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":37469,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"ff21a43b-ce04-4f26-bef6-b90685da5131"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Won2bZi9ced1kgzHagwBqu","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-01.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"368b2f30-bfc9-4c3e-a6d1-e6dd8149d956","timestamp":"2026-03-31T00:26:41.196Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-01.md","content":"## SLOT 1: Metals ranked by melting point\n\nThe three metals documented in the knowledge base are listed below in descending order of melting point, from highest to lowest. [@MetalsKB]\n\n1. Iron (Fe): $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCIron2024]\n2. Copper (Cu): $1085\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCCopper2024]\n3. Aluminum (Al): $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCAluminium2024]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U1RXBZ87mDd3Ungupm8J5u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SLOT_PROGRESS: slot-02 :: writing_output"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14408,"cache_read_input_tokens":53766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14408,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"a89db707-b97f-43fc-adc9-31f5f2354d26"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U1RXBZ87mDd3Ungupm8J5u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011R8Bc7nzJA2YTLrSx52hg7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-02.md","content":"## SLOT 2: Highest-density metal and structural relevance\n\nAmong the three metals in the knowledge base, copper exhibits the highest density at $8.96\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, exceeding both iron at $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$ and aluminum at $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$. [@MetalsKB][@RSCCopper2024]\n\nGreater mass per unit volume is the functional property that makes high-density materials applicable wherever concentrated mass within a constrained space is the primary design requirement. Dense metals are used as counterweights in aircraft control surfaces and crane assemblies, as ballast in marine vessels and offshore platforms, and as attenuation barriers in protective cladding, because their higher mass density provides stability and resistance to displacement in compact form. [@EngineerFix2024][@LKABMinerals2024] In radiation shielding, density governs the linear attenuation coefficient directly: higher-density materials interact with incident photons more frequently per unit thickness, permitting effective attenuation at reduced bulk volume. [@NIST2024][@MarShield2024] Stiffness and wear resistance in metallic materials are not simple functions of density; stiffness is governed primarily by atomic bond strength (quantified by the elastic modulus), and wear resistance correlates with hardness rather than density alone. [@MaterialProperties2024] Because the knowledge base documents only melting point and density for the three listed metals, quantitative comparison of their mechanical performance in load-bearing or high-contact applications requires property data beyond the current KB scope.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14408,"cache_read_input_tokens":53766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14408,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"7cad8a48-35c5-4da4-bcf3-c13d4d6c0ae8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011R8Bc7nzJA2YTLrSx52hg7","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-02.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"5952c2c6-4de6-4e9c-924d-c7eb80578a0c","timestamp":"2026-03-31T00:26:52.738Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-02.md","content":"## SLOT 2: Highest-density metal and structural relevance\n\nAmong the three metals in the knowledge base, copper exhibits the highest density at $8.96\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, exceeding both iron at $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$ and aluminum at $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$. [@MetalsKB][@RSCCopper2024]\n\nGreater mass per unit volume is the functional property that makes high-density materials applicable wherever concentrated mass within a constrained space is the primary design requirement. Dense metals are used as counterweights in aircraft control surfaces and crane assemblies, as ballast in marine vessels and offshore platforms, and as attenuation barriers in protective cladding, because their higher mass density provides stability and resistance to displacement in compact form. [@EngineerFix2024][@LKABMinerals2024] In radiation shielding, density governs the linear attenuation coefficient directly: higher-density materials interact with incident photons more frequently per unit thickness, permitting effective attenuation at reduced bulk volume. [@NIST2024][@MarShield2024] Stiffness and wear resistance in metallic materials are not simple functions of density; stiffness is governed primarily by atomic bond strength (quantified by the elastic modulus), and wear resistance correlates with hardness rather than density alone. [@MaterialProperties2024] Because the knowledge base documents only melting point and density for the three listed metals, quantitative comparison of their mechanical performance in load-bearing or high-contact applications requires property data beyond the current KB scope.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Gq9KFphvezndwox93FuM5n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SLOT_PROGRESS: slot-03 :: writing_output"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":560,"cache_read_input_tokens":68174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":560,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"87c34f71-69fa-4cbf-bf82-d8109024d4ce"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Gq9KFphvezndwox93FuM5n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JVLyXF8fwMd8qN1HPEE7Xb","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-03.md","content":"## SLOT 3: Iron versus aluminum comparison\n\nThe principal property differences between iron and aluminum, as documented in the knowledge base, are summarized below. [@MetalsKB]\n\n- **Melting point**: Iron melts at $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$; aluminum melts at $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, a difference of $878\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$. Iron exhibits substantially greater thermal stability under high-temperature conditions. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Density**: Iron has a density of $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$; aluminum has a density of $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, making aluminum approximately 2.9 times less dense per unit volume. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Atomic number**: Iron has atomic number 26; aluminum has atomic number 13, reflecting their respective positions in the periodic table. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":560,"cache_read_input_tokens":68174,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":560,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"05235215-85b2-490c-ba10-624865b96766"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JVLyXF8fwMd8qN1HPEE7Xb","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-03.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"62cea83e-563a-4cfb-bfd7-0ac231794d3b","timestamp":"2026-03-31T00:26:58.617Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.slot-03.md","content":"## SLOT 3: Iron versus aluminum comparison\n\nThe principal property differences between iron and aluminum, as documented in the knowledge base, are summarized below. [@MetalsKB]\n\n- **Melting point**: Iron melts at $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$; aluminum melts at $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, a difference of $878\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$. Iron exhibits substantially greater thermal stability under high-temperature conditions. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Density**: Iron has a density of $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$; aluminum has a density of $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, making aluminum approximately 2.9 times less dense per unit volume. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Atomic number**: Iron has atomic number 26; aluminum has atomic number 13, reflecting their respective positions in the periodic table. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U8owvo7nuGyzmfB8iDx4SG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHASE_PROGRESS: assembly :: reading_slot_outputs\n\nPHASE_PROGRESS: assembly :: writing_final_outputs"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":455,"cache_read_input_tokens":68734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":455,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"aabe4a64-8c07-4ad1-9b32-6e5a29fae12b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U8owvo7nuGyzmfB8iDx4SG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0129iaxqbrpKtALnHqy7NRde","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.md","content":"# Metals: Properties and Comparisons\n\n## SLOT 1: Metals ranked by melting point\n\nThe three metals documented in the knowledge base are listed below in descending order of melting point, from highest to lowest. [@MetalsKB]\n\n1. Iron (Fe): $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCIron2024]\n2. Copper (Cu): $1085\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCCopper2024]\n3. Aluminum (Al): $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCAluminium2024]\n\n## SLOT 2: Highest-density metal and structural relevance\n\nAmong the three metals in the knowledge base, copper exhibits the highest density at $8.96\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, exceeding both iron at $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$ and aluminum at $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$. [@MetalsKB][@RSCCopper2024]\n\nGreater mass per unit volume is the functional property that makes high-density materials applicable wherever concentrated mass within a constrained space is the primary design requirement. Dense metals are used as counterweights in aircraft control surfaces and crane assemblies, as ballast in marine vessels and offshore platforms, and as attenuation barriers in protective cladding, because their higher mass density provides stability and resistance to displacement in compact form. [@EngineerFix2024][@LKABMinerals2024] In radiation shielding, density governs the linear attenuation coefficient directly: higher-density materials interact with incident photons more frequently per unit thickness, permitting effective attenuation at reduced bulk volume. [@NIST2024][@MarShield2024] Stiffness and wear resistance in metallic materials are not simple functions of density; stiffness is governed primarily by atomic bond strength (quantified by the elastic modulus), and wear resistance correlates with hardness rather than density alone. [@MaterialProperties2024] Because the knowledge base documents only melting point and density for the three listed metals, quantitative comparison of their mechanical performance in load-bearing or high-contact applications requires property data beyond the current KB scope.\n\n## SLOT 3: Iron versus aluminum comparison\n\nThe principal property differences between iron and aluminum, as documented in the knowledge base, are summarized below. [@MetalsKB]\n\n- **Melting point**: Iron melts at $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$; aluminum melts at $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, a difference of $878\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$. Iron exhibits substantially greater thermal stability under high-temperature conditions. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Density**: Iron has a density of $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$; aluminum has a density of $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, making aluminum approximately 2.9 times less dense per unit volume. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Atomic number**: Iron has atomic number 26; aluminum has atomic number 13, reflecting their respective positions in the periodic table. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":455,"cache_read_input_tokens":68734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":455,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"7662d940-2528-4074-b756-923fb9142492"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0129iaxqbrpKtALnHqy7NRde","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4598beb5-2fb2-43ee-8b9d-9e35f34b3c9b","uuid":"56639f88-8913-4908-9217-d57467654ae8","timestamp":"2026-03-31T00:27:10.684Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/smoketest/sessions/run-20260330_212229/topo_comp_mesh.compile.research.md","content":"# Metals: Properties and Comparisons\n\n## SLOT 1: Metals ranked by melting point\n\nThe three metals documented in the knowledge base are listed below in descending order of melting point, from highest to lowest. [@MetalsKB]\n\n1. Iron (Fe): $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCIron2024]\n2. Copper (Cu): $1085\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCCopper2024]\n3. Aluminum (Al): $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ [@RSCAluminium2024]\n\n## SLOT 2: Highest-density metal and structural relevance\n\nAmong the three metals in the knowledge base, copper exhibits the highest density at $8.96\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, exceeding both iron at $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$ and aluminum at $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$. [@MetalsKB][@RSCCopper2024]\n\nGreater mass per unit volume is the functional property that makes high-density materials applicable wherever concentrated mass within a constrained space is the primary design requirement. Dense metals are used as counterweights in aircraft control surfaces and crane assemblies, as ballast in marine vessels and offshore platforms, and as attenuation barriers in protective cladding, because their higher mass density provides stability and resistance to displacement in compact form. [@EngineerFix2024][@LKABMinerals2024] In radiation shielding, density governs the linear attenuation coefficient directly: higher-density materials interact with incident photons more frequently per unit thickness, permitting effective attenuation at reduced bulk volume. [@NIST2024][@MarShield2024] Stiffness and wear resistance in metallic materials are not simple functions of density; stiffness is governed primarily by atomic bond strength (quantified by the elastic modulus), and wear resistance correlates with hardness rather than density alone. [@MaterialProperties2024] Because the knowledge base documents only melting point and density for the three listed metals, quantitative comparison of their mechanical performance in load-bearing or high-contact applications requires property data beyond the current KB scope.\n\n## SLOT 3: Iron versus aluminum comparison\n\nThe principal property differences between iron and aluminum, as documented in the knowledge base, are summarized below. [@MetalsKB]\n\n- **Melting point**: Iron melts at $1538\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$; aluminum melts at $660\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, a difference of $878\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$. Iron exhibits substantially greater thermal stability under high-temperature conditions. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Density**: Iron has a density of $7.874\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$; aluminum has a density of $2.70\\,\\text{g/cm}^3$, making aluminum approximately 2.9 times less dense per unit volume. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n- **Atomic number**: Iron has atomic number 26; aluminum has atomic number 13, reflecting their respective positions in the periodic table. [@RSCIron2024][@RSCAluminium2024]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
